==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
{{prefix|en|able}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɪˈneɪbəl/}}, {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ɛnˈeɪbəl/}}
* {{audio|en-us-enable.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|eɪbəl}}

===Verb===
{{wikipedia|enabling}}
{{en-verb|enabl|ing}}

# To give strength or ability to; to make firm and strong.
#* {{rfdate}}, ''[[King James Bible]]'', "1 Tim. i. 12"
#*: Who hath '''enabled''' me.
# To make able (to do, or to be, something); to confer sufficient power upon; to [[furnish]] with means, opportunities, and the like; to render competent for; to [[empower]]; to [[endow]].
#* '''1711''', October 13, {{w|Joseph Addison}}, ''{{w|The Spectator}}'', number 195
#*: Temperance gives Nature her full play, and '''enables''' her to exert herself in all her force and vigor.
#*{{quote-magazine|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=72-3|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/international/science-and-technology/21580133-how-microbes-promote-liver-cancer-overweight-punch-gut A punch in the gut]
|passage=Mostly, the microbiome is beneficial. It helps with digestion and '''enables''' people to extract a lot more calories from their food than would otherwise be possible. Research over the past few years, however, has implicated it in diseases from atherosclerosis to asthma to autism.}}
# To allow a way out or excuse for an action.
#: {{usex|lang=en|His parents '''enabled''' him to go on buying drugs.}}

